## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical consistency and latency guarantees across CNPA pipelines under load, focusing on real-world patterns beyond basic debugging.

## Key Concepts

- Transactional outbox across DB and Kafka
- Idempotent sinks and cache invalidation in Redis
- End-to-end correlation IDs and event timestamps
- Event-time processing and handling late events
- Canary rollouts and replay-based validation

## Code Example

```sql
-- Outbox pattern schema (example)
CREATE TABLE outbox (
  id BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
  aggregate_type TEXT NOT NULL,
  aggregate_id TEXT NOT NULL,
  payload JSONB NOT NULL,
  processed BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE,
  created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT now(),
  processed_at TIMESTAMPTZ
);
```

```javascript
// Pseudocode: propagate correlation and timestamp through HTTP and Kafka
function handleHttp(req) {
  const cid = req.headers['x-correlation-id'] || generateCid();
  req.correlationId = cid;
  // attach to Kafka message headers
  publishToKafka({
    headers: { 'correlation_id': cid, 'ts': Date.now() },
    body: req.body
  });
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test durability during partial outages and ensure no data loss?
- What metrics indicate freshness and how would you alert on drift?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to fuse transactional boundaries with event delivery and downstream idempotency, a common production risk area.

## Key Concepts

- Transactional outbox pattern across DB and message bus
- Idempotent Kafka producers and deduplication keys
- Downstream lake ingestion deduplication using ingest_id
- Failure/retry handling and exactly-once semantics

## Code Example

```sql
CREATE TABLE cnpa_outbox (
  id BIGINT PRIMARY KEY,
  aggregate_id UUID NOT NULL,
  event_type TEXT NOT NULL,
  payload JSONB NOT NULL,
  occurred_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL,
  processed BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE
);
```

```sql
BEGIN;
UPDATE cnpa_user SET name = $1 WHERE id = $2;
INSERT INTO cnpa_outbox (aggregate_id, event_type, payload, occurred_at)
VALUES ($3, 'UserUpdated', to_jsonb($4), NOW());
COMMIT;
```

```javascript
// Pseudo: outbox processor
while (row = fetchNextUnprocessed()) {
  producer.send({ topic: 'cnpa.user.updates', key: String(row.id), value: row.payload });
  markProcessed(row.id);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you monitor for outbox backlog and ensure exactly-once delivery under burst load?
- How would you test replay scenarios and ensure no duplicate lake records during schema changes?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of distributed tracing in CNPA stacks and practical, beginner-friendly instrumentation plans.

## Key Concepts
- Distributed tracing across HTTP, DB, Kafka, Redis
- Span propagation and naming, minimal overhead
- Sampling strategies and verification
- Reproducing latency with controlled bursts

## Code Example
```javascript
const { trace, context } = require('@opentelemetry/api');
const tracer = trace.getTracer('cnpa');
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const span = tracer.startSpan('http.request', { attributes: { method: req.method, url: req.url } }, context.active());
  res.on('finish', () => span.end());
  next();
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adjust sampling in production to reduce overhead?
- How would you validate that the identified slow span is resolved after changes?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to implement multi-tenant isolation in a production CNPA pipeline with zero-downtime rollouts, plus debugs for cross-service changes.

## Key Concepts
- Tenant isolation and topic routing
- Feature flags and canary rollout
- Kafka transactions, idempotent sinks, and deduplication
- Backward compatibility and rollback planning

## Code Example
```javascript
// routing sketch
function routeEvent(event){
  const tenant = event.headers?.['x-tenant-id'] ?? 'public';
  const topic = isTenantRoutingEnabled ? `tenant.${tenant}.events` : 'global.events';
  producer.send({ topic, key: event.id, value: encode(event) }, cb);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you detect and mitigate topic explosion?
- How would you validate no data loss during rollback?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes understanding of cache invalidation, versioned keys, outbox patterns, and end-to-end consistency in CNPA stacks. It also checks how to observe impact and plan safe rollouts.

## Key Concepts
- Cache invalidation strategies and TTLs
- Redis key versioning and cache-aside pattern
- Event-driven invalidation via Kafka
- Safe reads with read-fallbacks and refresh
- Observability of latency and hit ratios

## Code Example
```javascript
// Update path
BEGIN;
UPDATE profiles SET name=$1, email=$2, version = version + 1 WHERE id=$id;
COMMIT;
redis.del(`profile:${id}:v*`);
publishKafka('profile_updated', { id, version: NEW_VERSION });
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test race conditions when read happens concurrently with update?
- How would you tune TTLs and decide when to prune old keys?
